{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by jesse eisenberg. the film is based on the book \"the accidental billionaire\" by ben mezrich and tells the story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the legal battles that followed.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and former ceo of facebook. eisenberg's performance as zuckerberg is widely regarded as one of the best aspects of the film, bringing a depth and nuance to the character that is both captivating and unsettling. he is supported by a talented cast, including andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, joshua jackson as trey parker, and armie hammer as the winklevoss twins.\n\nthe film's portrayal of the early days of facebook and the relationships between its founders is both fascinating and unsettling. the film explores the complexities of the friendship between zuckerberg and saverin, and the subsequent betrayal and litigation that ensued. it also delves into the cutthroat world of silicon valley, where ideas are stolen and relationships are forged and broken with ease.\n\nfincher's direction is masterful, using a blend of fast-paced montages and more introspective scenes to bring the story to life. the film's cinematography is also noteworthy, capturing the frenetic energy of the silicon valley tech scene. the film's score, composed by trent reznor and atticus ross, is haunting and evocative, perfectly capturing the mood and tone of the film.\n\none of the most impressive aspects of the film is its ability to balance the personal and professional aspects of the story. the film is not just a biopic about the founding of facebook, but also a nuanced exploration of the personal relationships and motivations of its founders. this balance is expertly handled by fincher and eisenberg, who bring a level 